Delete meetings, stop reusing attendees, and add a Memo style

Three things that got in the way of using the app.

There was no way to get rid of a meeting. A false start leaves a folder holding
a WAV header and nothing else, and it stayed in the sidebar forever. Each row
now has a delete button that appears on hover, and an empty meeting is dimmed
and labelled so it reads as something to clear out rather than something lost.
Deleting always asks first, and the question names what would go with it - "30 s
of audio, a transcript and notes" - because that is the part worth knowing. It
goes to the recycle bin, not out: the one thing this app must never do is lose
audio. The handler refuses any path that is not a child of the meetings folder.

Attendees were persisted like a preference and prefilled into every new
meeting, which quietly put last week's names into today's notes and into the
transcription prompt. They are a fact about one meeting now: the field starts
empty, "New" clears it, finishing a recording clears it, and anything left over
in localStorage from the old behaviour is dropped on load. Style, detail and
instructions are still remembered, because those really are preferences.

Added a Memo note style, for a meeting you need to forward to someone who was
not there and will not ask follow-up questions: prose rather than bullets, a
Background section for the context they are missing, and wording that says
plainly when something was discussed but not settled instead of implying it was
agreed.

Verified against the real window: test-delete-ui.js drives the sidebar with a
stubbed confirmation and a throwaway Documents folder, checking that cancelling
deletes nothing, that only the clicked row goes, and that clicking delete does
not also open the meeting. test-options-ui.js checks the attendee field is
empty after a reload while the style survives it. test-memo.js runs a real
159 KB transcript through the real prompt and checks the Discussion section
comes back as prose with no bullets. test-meetings.js checks the path guard
against eight ways of trying to escape the meetings folder.

